  <title>Chairman's Letter - 1978</title>
</head>
<body>
<p align=3D"CENTER">
<b>BERKSHIRE HATHAWAY INC.</b>
</p>
<pre>
<i>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</i>

     First, a few words about accounting.  The merger with=20
Diversified Retailing Company, Inc. at yearend adds two new=20
complications in the presentation of our financial results. =20
After the merger, our ownership of Blue Chip Stamps increased to=20
approximately 58% and, therefore, the accounts of that company=20
must be fully consolidated in the Balance Sheet and Statement of=20
Earnings presentation of Berkshire.  In previous reports, our=20
share of the net earnings only of Blue Chip had been included as=20
a single item on Berkshire=92s Statement of Earnings, and there had=20
been a similar one-line inclusion on our Balance Sheet of our=20
share of their net assets.

     This full consolidation of sales, expenses, receivables,=20
inventories, debt, etc. produces an aggregation of figures from=20
many diverse businesses - textiles, insurance, candy, newspapers,=20
trading stamps - with dramatically different economic=20
characteristics.  In some of these your ownership is 100% but, in=20
those businesses which are owned by Blue Chip but fully=20
consolidated, your ownership as a Berkshire shareholder is only=20
58%. (Ownership by others of the balance of these businesses is=20
accounted for by the large minority interest item on the=20
liability side of the Balance Sheet.) Such a grouping of Balance=20
Sheet and Earnings items - some wholly owned, some partly owned -=20
tends to obscure economic reality more than illuminate it.  In=20
fact, it represents a form of presentation that we never prepare=20
for internal use during the year and which is of no value to us=20
in any management activities.

     For that reason, throughout the report we provide much=20
separate financial information and commentary on the various=20
segments of the business to help you evaluate Berkshire=92s=20
performance and prospects.  Much of this segmented information is=20
mandated by SEC disclosure rules and covered in =93Management=92s=20
Discussion=94 on pages 29 to 34.  And in this letter we try to=20
present to you a view of our various operating entities from the=20
same perspective that we view them managerially.

     A second complication arising from the merger is that the=20
1977 figures shown in this report are different from the 1977=20
figures shown in the report we mailed to you last year. =20
Accounting convention requires that when two entities such as=20
Diversified and Berkshire are merged, all financial data=20
subsequently must be presented as if the companies had been=20
merged at the time they were formed rather than just recently. =20
So the enclosed financial statements, in effect, pretend that in=20
1977 (and earlier years) the Diversified-Berkshire merger already=20
had taken place, even though the actual merger date was December=20
30, 1978.  This shifting base makes comparative commentary=20
confusing and, from time to time in our narrative report, we will=20
talk of figures and performance for Berkshire shareholders as=20
historically reported to you rather than as restated after the=20
Diversified merger.

     With that preamble it can be stated that, with or without=20
restated figures, 1978 was a good year.  Operating earnings,=20
exclusive of capital gains, at 19.4% of beginning shareholders=92=20
investment were within a fraction of our 1972 record.  While we=20
believe it is improper to include capital gains or losses in=20
evaluating the performance of a single year, they are an=20
important component of the longer term record.  Because of such=20
gains, Berkshire=92s long-term growth in equity per share has been=20
greater than would be indicated by compounding the returns from=20
operating earnings that we have reported annually.

     For example, over the last three years - generally a bonanza=20
period for the insurance industry, our largest profit producer -=20
Berkshire=92s per share net worth virtually has doubled, thereby=20
compounding at about 25% annually through a combination of good=20
operating earnings and fairly substantial capital gains.  Neither=20
this 25% equity gain from all sources nor the 19.4% equity gain=20
from operating earnings in 1978 is sustainable.  The insurance=20
cycle has turned downward in 1979, and it is almost certain that=20
operating earnings measured by return on equity will fall this=20
year.  However, operating earnings measured in dollars are likely=20
to increase on the much larger shareholders=92 equity now employed=20
in the business.

     In contrast to this cautious view about near term return=20
from operations, we are optimistic about prospects for long term=20
return from major equity investments held by our insurance=20
companies.  We make no attempt to predict how security markets=20
will behave; successfully forecasting short term stock price=20
movements is something we think neither we nor anyone else can=20
do.  In the longer run, however, we feel that many of our major=20
equity holdings are going to be worth considerably more money=20
than we paid, and that investment gains will add significantly to=20
the operating returns of the insurance group.

