     Blue Chip and Wesco are public companies with reporting=20
requirements of their own.  Later in this report we are=20
reproducing the narrative reports of the principal executives of=20
both companies, describing their 1978 operations.  Some of the=20
figures they utilize will not match to the penny the ones we use=20
in this report, again because of accounting and tax complexities. =20
But their comments should be helpful to you in understanding the=20
underlying economic characteristics of these important partly-
owned businesses.  A copy of the full annual report of either=20
company will be mailed to any shareholder of Berkshire upon=20
request to Mr. Robert H. Bird for Blue Chips Stamps, 5801 South=20
Eastern Avenue, Los Angeles, California 90040, or to Mrs. Bette=20
Deckard for Wesco Financial Corporation, 315 East Colorado=20
Boulevard, Pasadena, California 91109.

<i>Textiles</i>

     Earnings of $1.3 million in 1978, while much improved from=20
1977, still represent a low return on the $17 million of capital=20
employed in this business.  Textile plant and equipment are on=20
the books for a very small fraction of what it would cost to=20
replace such equipment today.  And, despite the age of the=20
equipment, much of it is functionally similar to new equipment=20
being installed by the industry.  But despite this =93bargain cost=94=20
of fixed assets, capital turnover is relatively low reflecting=20
required high investment levels in receivables and inventory=20
compared to sales.  Slow capital turnover, coupled with low=20
profit margins on sales, inevitably produces inadequate returns=20
on capital.  Obvious approaches to improved profit margins=20
involve differentiation of product, lowered manufacturing costs=20
through more efficient equipment or better utilization of people,=20
redirection toward fabrics enjoying stronger market trends, etc. =20
Our management is diligent in pursuing such objectives.  The=20
problem, of course, is that our competitors are just as=20
diligently doing the same thing.

     The textile industry illustrates in textbook style how=20
producers of relatively undifferentiated goods in capital=20
intensive businesses must earn inadequate returns except under=20
conditions of tight supply or real shortage.  As long as excess=20
productive capacity exists, prices tend to reflect direct=20
operating costs rather than capital employed.  Such a supply-
excess condition appears likely to prevail most of the time in=20
the textile industry, and our expectations are for profits of=20
relatively modest amounts in relation to capital.

     We hope we don=92t get into too many more businesses with such=20
tough economic characteristics.  But, as we have stated before:=20
(1) our textile businesses are very important employers in their=20
communities, (2) management has been straightforward in reporting=20
on problems and energetic in attacking them, (3) labor has been=20
cooperative and understanding in facing our common problems, and=20
(4) the business should average modest cash returns relative to=20
investment.  As long as these conditions prevail - and we expect=20
that they will - we intend to continue to support our textile=20
business despite more attractive alternative uses for capital.

<i>Insurance Underwriting</i>

     The number one contributor to Berkshire=92s overall excellent=20
results in 1978 was the segment of National Indemnity Company=92s=20
insurance operation run by Phil Liesche.  On about $90 million of=20
earned premiums, an underwriting profit of approximately $11=20
million was realized, a truly extraordinary achievement even=20
against the background of excellent industry conditions.  Under=20
Phil=92s leadership, with outstanding assistance by Roland Miller=20
in Underwriting and Bill Lyons in Claims, this segment of=20
National Indemnity (including National Fire and Marine Insurance=20
Company, which operates as a running mate) had one of its best=20
years in a long history of performances which, in aggregate, far=20
outshine those of the industry.  Present successes reflect credit=20
not only upon present managers, but equally upon the business=20
talents of Jack Ringwalt, founder of National Indemnity, whose=20
operating philosophy remains etched upon the company.

     Home and Automobile Insurance Company had its best year=20
since John Seward stepped in and straightened things out in 1975. =20
Its results are combined in this report with those of Phil=20
Liesche=92s operation under the insurance category entitled=20
=93Specialized Auto and General Liability=94.

     Worker=92s Compensation was a mixed bag in 1978.  In its first=20
year as a subsidiary, Cypress Insurance Company, managed by Milt=20
Thornton, turned in outstanding results.  The worker=92s=20
compensation line can cause large underwriting losses when rapid=20
inflation interacts with changing social concepts, but Milt has a=20
cautious and highly professional staff to cope with these=20
problems.  His performance in 1978 has reinforced our very good=20
feelings about this purchase.

     Frank DeNardo came with us in the spring of 1978 to=20
straighten out National Indemnity=92s California Worker=92s=20
Compensation business which, up to that point, had been a=20
disaster.  Frank has the experience and intellect needed to=20
correct the major problems of the Los Angeles office.  Our volume=20
in this department now is running only about 25% of what it was=20
eighteen months ago, and early indications are that Frank is=20
making good progress.

     George Young=92s reinsurance department continues to produce=20
very large sums for investment relative to premium volume, and=20
thus gives us reasonably satisfactory overall results.  However,=20
underwriting results still are not what they should be and can=20
be.  It is very easy to fool yourself regarding underwriting=20
results in reinsurance (particularly in casualty lines involving=20
long delays in settlement), and we believe this situation=20
prevails with many of our competitors.  Unfortunately, self-
delusion in company reserving almost always leads to inadequate=20
industry rate levels.  If major factors in the market don=92t know=20
their true costs, the competitive =93fall-out=94 hits all - even=20
those with adequate cost knowledge.  George is quite willing to=20
reduce volume significantly, if needed, to achieve satisfactory=20
underwriting, and we have a great deal of confidence in the long=20
term soundness of this business under his direction.

     The homestate operation was disappointing in 1978.  Our=20
unsatisfactory underwriting, even though partially explained by=20
an unusual incidence of Midwestern storms, is particularly=20
worrisome against the backdrop of very favorable industry results=20
in the conventional lines written by our homestate group.  We=20
have confidence in John Ringwalt=92s ability to correct this=20
situation.  The bright spot in the group was the performance of=20
Kansas Fire and Casualty in its first full year of business. =20
Under Floyd Taylor, this subsidiary got off to a truly remarkable=20
start.  Of course, it takes at least several years to evaluate=20
underwriting results, but the early signs are encouraging and=20
Floyd=92s operation achieved the best loss ratio among the=20
homestate companies in 1978.

     Although some segments were disappointing, overall our=20
insurance operation had an excellent year.  But of course we=20
should expect a good year when the industry is flying high, as in=20
1978.  It is a virtual certainty that in 1979 the combined ratio=20
(see definition on page 31) for the industry will move up at=20
least a few points, perhaps enough to throw the industry as a=20
whole into an underwriting loss position.  For example, in the=20
auto lines - by far the most important area for the industry and=20
for us - CPI figures indicate rates overall were only 3% higher=20
in January 1979 than a year ago.  But the items that make up loss=20
costs - auto repair and medical care costs - were up over 9%. =20
How different than yearend 1976 when rates had advanced over 22%=20
in the preceding twelve months, but costs were up 8%.

     Margins will remain steady only if rates rise as fast as=20
costs.  This assuredly will not be the case in 1979, and=20
conditions probably will worsen in 1980.  Our present thinking is=20
that our underwriting performance relative to the industry will=20
improve somewhat in 1979, but every other insurance management=20
probably views its relative prospects with similar optimism -=20
someone is going to be disappointed.  Even if we do improve=20
relative to others, we may well have a higher combined ratio and=20
lower underwriting profits in 1979 than we achieved last year.

     We continue to look for ways to expand our insurance=20
operation.  But your reaction to this intent should not be=20
unrestrained joy.  Some of our expansion efforts - largely=20
initiated by your Chairman have been lackluster, others have been=20
expensive failures.  We entered the business in 1967 through=20
purchase of the segment which Phil Liesche now manages, and it=20
still remains, by a large margin, the best portion of our=20
insurance business.  It is not easy to buy a good insurance=20
business, but our experience has been that it is easier to buy=20
one than create one.  However, we will continue to try both=20
approaches, since the rewards for success in this field can be=20
exceptional.

<i>Insurance Investments</i>

     We confess considerable optimism regarding our insurance=20
equity investments.  Of course, our enthusiasm for stocks is not=20
unconditional.  Under some circumstances, common stock=20
investments by insurers make very little sense.

     We get excited enough to commit a big percentage of=20
insurance company net worth to equities only when we find (1)=20
businesses we can understand, (2) with favorable long-term=20
prospects, (3) operated by honest and competent people, and (4)=20
priced very attractively.  We usually can identify a small number=20
of potential investments meeting requirements (1), (2) and (3),=20
but (4) often prevents action.  For example, in 1971 our total=20
common stock position at Berkshire=92s insurance subsidiaries=20
amounted to only $10.7 million at cost, and $11.7 million at=20
market.  There were equities of identifiably excellent companies=20
available - but very few at interesting prices. (An irresistible=20
footnote: in 1971, pension fund managers invested a record 122%=20
of net funds available in equities - at full prices they couldn=92t=20
buy enough of them.  In 1974, after the bottom had fallen out,=20
they committed a then record low of 21% to stocks.)

     The past few years have been a different story for us.  At=20
the end of 1975 our insurance subsidiaries held common equities=20
with a market value exactly equal to cost of $39.3 million.  At=20
the end of 1978 this position had been increased to equities=20
(including a convertible preferred) with a cost of $129.1 million=20
and a market value of $216.5 million.  During the intervening=20
three years we also had realized pre-tax gains from common=20
equities of approximately $24.7 million.  Therefore, our overall=20
unrealized and realized pre-tax gains in equities for the three=20
year period came to approximately $112 million.  During this same=20
interval the Dow-Jones Industrial Average declined from 852 to=20
805.  It was a marvelous period for the value-oriented equity=20
buyer.

     We continue to find for our insurance portfolios small=20
portions of really outstanding businesses that are available,=20
through the auction pricing mechanism of security markets, at=20
prices dramatically cheaper than the valuations inferior=20
businesses command on negotiated sales.

     This program of acquisition of small fractions of businesses=20
(common stocks) at bargain prices, for which little enthusiasm=20
exists, contrasts sharply with general corporate acquisition=20
activity, for which much enthusiasm exists.  It seems quite clear=20
to us that either corporations are making very significant=20
mistakes in purchasing entire businesses at prices prevailing in=20
negotiated transactions and takeover bids, or that we eventually=20
are going to make considerable sums of money buying small=20
portions of such businesses at the greatly discounted valuations=20
prevailing in the stock market. (A second footnote: in 1978=20
pension managers, a group that logically should maintain the=20
longest of investment perspectives, put only 9% of net available=20
funds into equities - breaking the record low figure set in 1974=20
and tied in 1977.)

     We are not concerned with whether the market quickly=20
revalues upward securities that we believe are selling at bargain=20
prices.  In fact, we prefer just the opposite since, in most=20
years, we expect to have funds available to be a net buyer of=20
securities.  And consistent attractive purchasing is likely to=20
prove to be of more eventual benefit to us than any selling=20
opportunities provided by a short-term run up in stock prices to=20
levels at which we are unwilling to continue buying.

     Our policy is to concentrate holdings.  We try to avoid=20
buying a little of this or that when we are only lukewarm about=20
the business or its price.  When we are convinced as to=20
attractiveness, we believe in buying worthwhile amounts.

