     In some cases our indirect interest in earning power is=20
becoming quite substantial.  For example, note our holdings of=20
953,750 shares of SAFECO Corp. SAFECO probably is the best run=20
large property and casualty insurance company in the United=20
States.  Their underwriting abilities are simply superb, their=20
loss reserving is conservative, and their investment policies=20
make great sense.

     SAFECO is a much better insurance operation than our own=20
(although we believe certain segments of ours are much better=20
than average), is better than one we could develop and,=20
similarly, is far better than any in which we might negotiate=20
purchase of a controlling interest.  Yet our purchase of SAFECO=20
was made at substantially under book value.  We paid less than=20
100 cents on the dollar for the best company in the business,=20
when far more than 100 cents on the dollar is being paid for=20
mediocre companies in corporate transactions.  And there is no=20
way to start a new operation - with necessarily uncertain=20
prospects - at less than 100 cents on the dollar.

     Of course, with a minor interest we do not have the right to=20
direct or even influence management policies of SAFECO.  But why=20
should we wish to do this?  The record would indicate that they=20
do a better job of managing their operations than we could do=20
ourselves.  While there may be less excitement and prestige in=20
sitting back and letting others do the work, we think that is all=20
one loses by accepting a passive participation in excellent=20
management.  Because, quite clearly, if one controlled a company=20
run as well as SAFECO, the proper policy also would be to sit=20
back and let management do its job.

     Earnings attributable to the shares of SAFECO owned by=20
Berkshire at yearend amounted to $6.1 million during 1978, but=20
only the dividends received (about 18% of earnings) are reflected=20
in our operating earnings.  We believe the balance, although not=20
reportable, to be just as real in terms of eventual benefit to us=20
as the amount distributed.  In fact, SAFECO=92s retained earnings=20
(or those of other well-run companies if they have opportunities=20
to employ additional capital advantageously) may well eventually=20
have a value to shareholders greater than 100 cents on the=20
dollar.

     We are not at all unhappy when our wholly-owned businesses=20
retain all of their earnings if they can utilize internally those=20
funds at attractive rates.  Why should we feel differently about=20
retention of earnings by companies in which we hold small equity=20
interests, but where the record indicates even better prospects=20
for profitable employment of capital? (This proposition cuts the=20
other way, of course, in industries with low capital=20
requirements, or if management has a record of plowing capital=20
into projects of low profitability; then earnings should be paid=20
out or used to repurchase shares - often by far the most=20
attractive option for capital utilization.)

     The aggregate level of such retained earnings attributable=20
to our equity interests in fine companies is becoming quite=20
substantial.  It does not enter into our reported operating=20
earnings, but we feel it well may have equal long-term=20
significance to our shareholders.  Our hope is that conditions=20
continue to prevail in securities markets which allow our=20
insurance companies to buy large amounts of underlying earning=20
power for relatively modest outlays.  At some point market=20
conditions undoubtedly will again preclude such bargain buying=20
but, in the meantime, we will try to make the most of=20
opportunities.

<i>Banking</i>

     Under Gene Abegg and Pete Jeffrey, the Illinois National=20
Bank and Trust Company in Rockford continues to establish new=20
records.  Last year=92s earnings amounted to approximately 2.1% of=20
average assets, about three times the level averaged by major=20
banks.  In our opinion, this extraordinary level of earnings is=20
being achieved while maintaining significantly less asset risk=20
than prevails at most of the larger banks.

     We purchased the Illinois National Bank in March 1969.  It=20
was a first-class operation then, just as it had been ever since=20
Gene Abegg opened the doors in 1931.  Since 1968, consumer time=20
deposits have quadrupled, net income has tripled and trust=20
department income has more than doubled, while costs have been=20
closely controlled.

     Our experience has been that the manager of an already high-
cost operation frequently is uncommonly resourceful in finding=20
new ways to add to overhead, while the manager of a tightly-run=20
operation usually continues to find additional methods to curtail=20
costs, even when his costs are already well below those of his=20
competitors.  No one has demonstrated this latter ability better=20
than Gene Abegg.

     We are required to divest our bank by December 31, 1980. =20
The most likely approach is to spin it off to Berkshire=20
shareholders some time in the second half of 1980.

<i>Retailing</i>

     Upon merging with Diversified, we acquired 100% ownership of=20
Associated Retail Stores, Inc., a chain of about 75 popular=20
priced women=92s apparel stores.  Associated was launched in=20
Chicago on March 7, 1931 with one store, $3200, and two=20
extraordinary partners, Ben Rosner and Leo Simon.  After Mr.=20
Simon=92s death, the business was offered to Diversified for cash=20
in 1967.  Ben was to continue running the business - and run it,=20
he has.

     Associated=92s business has not grown, and it consistently has=20
faced adverse demographic and retailing trends.  But Ben=92s=20
combination of merchandising, real estate and cost-containment=20
skills has produced an outstanding record of profitability, with=20
returns on capital necessarily employed in the business often in=20
the 20% after-tax area.

     Ben is now 75 and, like Gene Abegg, 81, at Illinois National=20
and Louie Vincenti, 73, at Wesco, continues daily to bring an=20
almost passionately proprietary attitude to the business.  This=20
group of top managers must appear to an outsider to be an=20
overreaction on our part to an OEO bulletin on age=20
discrimination.  While unorthodox, these relationships have been=20
exceptionally rewarding, both financially and personally.  It is=20
a real pleasure to work with managers who enjoy coming to work=20
each morning and, once there, instinctively and unerringly think=20
like owners.  We are associated with some of the very best.


                                    Warren E. Buffett, Chairman

